1
00:00:01,271 --> 00:00:12,071
(Q31) As we saw in the last lecture, a major cause of climate change is the rapid rise in the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere over the last century.

2
00:00:12,071 --> 00:00:19,319
If we could reduce the amount of CO2, perhaps the rate of climate change could also be slowed down.

3
00:00:19,319 --> 00:00:28,008
One potential method involves enhancing the role of the soil that plants grow in, with regard to absorbing CO2.

4
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:46,296
[Q31跟定位： 小标题] Rattan Lal , a soil scientist from Ohio State University, in the USA, claims that the world's agricultural soils could potentially absorb 13 percent of the carbon dioxide in the atmosphere - the equivalent of the amount released in the last 30 years.

5
00:00:46,296 --> 00:00:50,521
And research is going on into how this might be achieved.

6
00:00:50,521 --> 00:01:03,620
Lal first came to the idea that soil might be valuable in this way not through an interest in climate change, but rather out of concern for the land itself and the people dependent on it.&nbsp;

7
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:10,474
Carbon-rich soil is dark, crumbly and fertile, and retains some water.

8
00:01:10,474 --> 00:01:18,993
[Q31答案句【dry】， 跟定位词 erosion ， 疑似答案记录] But erosion can occur if soil is dry, which is a likely effect if it contains inadequate amounts of carbon.

9
00:01:18,993 --> 00:01:25,965
Erosion is of course bad for people trying to grow crops or breed animals on that terrain.

10
00:01:25,965 --> 00:01:37,961
(Q32) [Africa定位词，Q32答案句【hard】] In the 1970s and '80s, Lai was studying soils in Africa so devoid of organic matter that the around had become extremely hard, like cement.&nbsp;

11
00:01:37,961 --> 00:01:47,009
(Q33) There he met a pioneer in the study of global warming, who suggested that carbon from the soil had moved into the atmosphere.

12
00:01:47,009 --> 00:01:50,372
This is now looking increasingly likely.

13
00:01:50,372 --> 00:01:52,243
Let me explain.

14
00:01:52,243 --> 00:02:05,152
For millions of years, carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been regulated, in part, by a natural partnership between plants and microbes - tiny organisms in the soil.

15
00:02:05,152 --> 00:02:13,532
[Q33答案句，CO2 from the air 原词， transform= turn ... into..., 连接词  A such as B = B and A] Plants absorb CO2 from the air and transform it into sugars and other carbon-based substances.

16
00:02:13,532 --> 00:02:25,572
(Q34) [Q34答案句【roots】， some、from原词，transfer=move] While a proportion of these carbon products remain in the plant, some transfer from the roots to fungi and soil microbes, which store the carbon in the soil.

17
00:02:25,572 --> 00:02:37,131
(Q35) [定位 loss of carbon 原词， 后面都是在解释] The invention of agriculture some 10,000 years ago disrupted these ancient soil-building processes and led to the loss of carbon from the soil.

18
00:02:37,131 --> 00:02:46,757
When humans started draining the natural topsoil, and ploughing it up for planting, they exposed the buried carbon to oxygen.

19
00:02:46,757 --> 00:02:51,608
This created carbon dioxide and released it into the air.

20
00:02:51,608 --> 00:03:00,619
And in some places, grazing by domesticated animals has removed all vegetation, releasing carbon into the air.

21
00:03:00,619 --> 00:03:08,624
[be stripped from = lost] Tons of carbon have been stripped from the world's soils - where it's needed - and pumped into the atmosphere.

22
00:03:08,624 --> 00:03:11,160
So what can be done?

23
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:21,612
Researchers are now coming up with evidence that even modest changes to farming can significantly help to reduce the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.

24
00:03:21,612 --> 00:03:27,992
[Q35跟定位小标题， 中间隔了很多] Some growers have already started using an approach known as regenerative agriculture.

25
00:03:27,992 --> 00:03:35,261
[Q35答案句] This aims to boost the fertility of soil and keep it moist through established practices.

26
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:43,065
(Q36) [this includes = e.g.] These include keeping fields planted all year round, and increasing the variety of plants being grown.

27
00:03:43,065 --> 00:03:59,443
(Q37) Strategies like these can significantly increase the amount of carbon stored in the soil, so agricultural researchers are now building a case for their use in combating climate change.

28
00:03:59,443 --> 00:04:09,130
[Q37跟定位 California] One American investigation into the potential for storing CO2 on agricultural lands is taking place in California.

29
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:20,180
[Q37答案句： ... conduct = take place , large=big] Soil scientist Whendee Silver of the University of California, Berkeley, is conducting a first-of-its-kind study on a large cattle farm in the state.

30
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:37,281
(Q38) [Q38答案句] She and her students are testing the effects on carbon storage of the compost that is created from waste - both agricultural, including manure and cornstalks, and waste produced in gardens, such as leaves, branches, and lawn trimmings.

31
00:04:37,281 --> 00:04:45,796
(Q39) In Australia, soil ecologist Christine Jones is testing another promising soil-enrichment strategy.

32
00:04:45,796 --> 00:04:55,289
[Q39答案句， cultivate = use] Jones and 12 farmers are working to build up soil carbon by cultivating grasses that stay green all year round.

33
00:04:55,289 --> 00:05:09,136
(Q40) Like composting, the approach has already been proved experimentally; Jones now hopes to show that it can be applied on working farms and that the resulting carbon capture can be accurately measured.

34
00:05:09,136 --> 00:05:21,120
[Q40跟定位 future] It's hoped in the future that projects such as these will demonstrate the role that farmers and other land managers can play in reducing the harmful effects of greenhouse gases.

35
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:34,250
[application of fertilizer = fertilizer used in ...] For example, in countries like the United States, where most farming operations use large applications of fertiliser, changing such long-standing habits will require a change of system.

36
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:45,500
[Q40答案句： farmer原词，  farmer should receive = give farmers ...] Rattan Lal argues that farmers should receive payment not just for the corn or beef they produce, but also for the carbon they can store in their soil.&nbsp;

37
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,000
Another study being carried out ...

